The board is filled row by row, while three boolean arrays keep track of used columns and diagonals. The diagonal indexes are made from `row + col` and `row - col + n - 1`, so each check is constant time.

After recursion returns, all three marks are cleared. This reset is needed before trying the next column.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<String>> solveNQueens(int n) {
        List<List<String>> answers = new ArrayList<>();
        if (n <= 0) {
            return answers;
        }

        char[][] board = new char[n][n];
        for (int row = 0; row < n; row++) {
            Arrays.fill(board[row], '.');
        }

        boolean[] usedColumns = new boolean[n];
        boolean[] usedDiagonals = new boolean[2 * n - 1];
        boolean[] usedOtherDiagonals = new boolean[2 * n - 1];

        search(0, board, usedColumns, usedDiagonals,
               usedOtherDiagonals, answers);
        return answers;
    }

    private void search(int row, char[][] board, boolean[] usedColumns,
                        boolean[] usedDiagonals,
                        boolean[] usedOtherDiagonals,
                        List<List<String>> answers) {
        int n = board.length;

        if (row == n) {
            List<String> current = new ArrayList<>();
            for (char[] boardRow : board) {
                current.add(new String(boardRow));
            }
            answers.add(current);
            return;
        }

        for (int col = 0; col < n; col++) {
            int diagonal = row + col;
            int otherDiagonal = row - col + n - 1;

            if (usedColumns[col] || usedDiagonals[diagonal]
                    || usedOtherDiagonals[otherDiagonal]) {
                continue;
            }

            board[row][col] = 'Q';
            usedColumns[col] = true;
            usedDiagonals[diagonal] = true;
            usedOtherDiagonals[otherDiagonal] = true;

            search(row + 1, board, usedColumns, usedDiagonals,
                   usedOtherDiagonals, answers);

            board[row][col] = '.';
            usedColumns[col] = false;
            usedDiagonals[diagonal] = false;
            usedOtherDiagonals[otherDiagonal] = false;
        }
    }
}
```

For `n = 1`, the only cell is accepted and the result is `[["Q"]]`. For `n = 2` and `n = 3`, the search finishes without adding a board, which correctly gives an empty list.

Time complexity: O(n!). The conflict checks are O(1), not full board scans.

Space complexity: O(n^2), not counting the output. This includes the reusable board, tracking arrays, and recursion stack.

Instead of changing a full board during the search, this version stores only the queen column for each row. `positions[row]` tells where the queen is in that row. A candidate is valid when it does not share a column or diagonal with any earlier row.

The board strings are created only after all row positions are valid. This keeps the search state small and makes the placement rule easy to see.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<String>> solveNQueens(int n) {
        List<List<String>> answers = new ArrayList<>();
        if (n <= 0) {
            return answers;
        }

        int[] positions = new int[n];
        Arrays.fill(positions, -1);
        placeRow(0, positions, answers);
        return answers;
    }

    private void placeRow(int row, int[] positions,
                           List<List<String>> answers) {
        int n = positions.length;

        if (row == n) {
            List<String> board = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int currentRow = 0; currentRow < n; currentRow++) {
                char[] line = new char[n];
                Arrays.fill(line, '.');
                line[positions[currentRow]] = 'Q';
                board.add(new String(line));
            }
            answers.add(board);
            return;
        }

        for (int col = 0; col < n; col++) {
            if (!canPlace(row, col, positions)) {
                continue;
            }

            positions[row] = col;
            placeRow(row + 1, positions, answers);
            positions[row] = -1;
        }
    }

    private boolean canPlace(int row, int col, int[] positions) {
        for (int previousRow = 0; previousRow < row; previousRow++) {
            int previousCol = positions[previousRow];

            if (previousCol == col
                    || Math.abs(previousRow - row)
                       == Math.abs(previousCol - col)) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

For `n = 4`, one successful path is columns `1, 3, 0, 2`. That becomes:

```text
.Q..
...Q
Q...
..Q.
```

The recursion then resets the last position and keeps searching for the other arrangement. For `n = 1`, `positions[0]` becomes 0 and the generated board is `Q`.

Time complexity: O(n * n!). Each candidate compares with earlier rows, which can take O(n).

Space complexity: O(n) for the position array and recursion, excluding the output boards.

I place one queen in each row and try every column. Because lower rows are still empty, a candidate only needs checks against earlier rows: the same column and the two upper diagonals.

When a later row has no valid position, the last queen is removed and another column is tried. The board is changed during the search, then copied when a full arrangement is found.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<string>> solveNQueens(int n) {
        vector<vector<string>> answers;
        if (n <= 0) {
            return answers;
        }

        vector<string> board(n, string(n, '.'));
        placeQueens(board, 0, answers);
        return answers;
    }

private:
    void placeQueens(vector<string>& board, int row,
                     vector<vector<string>>& answers) {
        int n = board.size();

        if (row == n) {
            answers.push_back(board);
            return;
        }

        for (int col = 0; col < n; ++col) {
            if (!isSafe(board, row, col)) {
                continue;
            }

            board[row][col] = 'Q';
            placeQueens(board, row + 1, answers);
            board[row][col] = '.';
        }
    }

    bool isSafe(const vector<string>& board, int row, int col) {
        for (int previousRow = 0; previousRow < row; ++previousRow) {
            if (board[previousRow][col] == 'Q') {
                return false;
            }
        }

        for (int r = row - 1, c = col - 1; r >= 0 && c >= 0; --r, --c) {
            if (board[r][c] == 'Q') {
                return false;
            }
        }

        for (int r = row - 1, c = col + 1;
             r >= 0 && c < static_cast<int>(board.size());
             --r, ++c) {
            if (board[r][c] == 'Q') {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
};
```

For a 4 by 4 board, one branch starts with column 0 in row 0. It later gets stuck, so the queen is removed and the search tries another column. The two boards that survive are recorded.

Time complexity: O(n * n!). The safety scan can take O(n) for each explored placement.

Space complexity: O(n^2), excluding the returned boards. The board uses O(n^2) space and the recursion uses O(n) space.

This version stores occupied columns and diagonals inside integers. A set bit means that position is already used. The available positions in a row are found with a bit operation, so there is no loop over blocked columns.

The board still uses strings because the platform wants every complete board in that format. The bitmask values are passed by value, so the recursive call gets its own updated state and no manual mask undo is needed.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<string>> solveNQueens(int n) {
        vector<vector<string>> answers;
        if (n <= 0) {
            return answers;
        }

        vector<string> board(n, string(n, '.'));
        int allColumns = (1 << n) - 1;
        search(0, n, allColumns, 0, 0, 0, board, answers);
        return answers;
    }

private:
    void search(int row, int n, int allColumns, int columns,
                int diagonals, int otherDiagonals,
                vector<string>& board,
                vector<vector<string>>& answers) {
        if (row == n) {
            answers.push_back(board);
            return;
        }

        int available = allColumns & ~(columns | diagonals | otherDiagonals);

        while (available != 0) {
            int bit = available & -available;
            available -= bit;

            int col = __builtin_ctz(bit);
            board[row][col] = 'Q';

            search(row + 1, n, allColumns,
                   columns | bit,
                   ((diagonals | bit) << 1) & allColumns,
                   (otherDiagonals | bit) >> 1,
                   board, answers);

            board[row][col] = '.';
        }
    }
};
```

The masks are kept to the lowest `n` bits with `allColumns`. The left and right diagonal masks shift when moving to the next row, which matches how diagonal attacks move across the board.

For `n = 1`, `allColumns` has only its lowest bit set and one board is returned. For `n = 2` or `n = 3`, no complete mask reaches the final row, so the answer is empty.

Time complexity: O(n!) in the usual backtracking bound, with very small conflict checks.

Space complexity: O(n^2) for the board and stored output, plus O(n) recursion depth. The mask state itself uses constant space.

